"""Prepared reference solution for FlagGems ``norm_scalar`` (aten::norm.Scalar).

Master workload this wrapper has to reproduce
(``tests/test_norm.py::test_norm_scalar``)::

    ref_out = torch.ops.aten.norm.Scalar(ref_inp)   # ref_inp = to_reference(inp, True)
    res_out = flag_gems.norm_scalar(inp)            # candidate ABI
    gems_assert_close(res_out, ref_out, dtype)

so the oracle is the native ``aten::norm.Scalar`` kernel over *all* elements
(``p`` defaults to 2), returning a new 0-dim tensor with the input dtype,
without mutating or aliasing the input (``definitions/norm_scalar.json``:
``parameters`` x (required) / p (optional, default 2), ``outputs`` ["out"],
``effects`` mutates=[] / returns_alias_of={}).

The candidate ABI seen at the callsites is ``norm_scalar(x)`` in accuracy and
``norm_scalar(x)`` in the benchmark; ``p`` is kept because the frozen
Definition exposes it.

This module is the file handed to the existing ``--override`` injection
(``norm_scalar:<this file>:run``) and is also the ``--reference-source``. It
uses only native ATen: the candidate's own ``norm_scalar`` export
(``flag_gems.norm_scalar``) is never imported or called as an oracle.

No ``flag_gems.use_gems()`` scope is active at either gated callsite (the
accuracy test calls the export directly; the benchmark invokes the injected
override under a ``nullcontext``), so ``aten::norm.Scalar`` here is the native
kernel and cannot re-enter the injected candidate.
"""

import torch


def run(x, p=2):
    """Return the p-norm (default 2-norm) of all elements of ``x`` as a 0-dim tensor."""
    if p is None or p == 2:
        return torch.ops.aten.norm.Scalar(x)
    return torch.ops.aten.norm.Scalar(x, p)
